{"causes":["Stephen Hawking's struggles with motor neuron disease led him to reexamine the fundamental laws governing the universe.","The early 20th-century 'Eternal Universe' hypothesis sparked debate among physicists about the nature of time and space.","Newtonian mechanics, underpinning classical physics, began to show limitations in explaining phenomena at the atomic and subatomic level."],"effects":["A Brief History of Time sparked a renewed interest in theoretical physics and cosmology, inspiring new areas of research and discovery.","Hawking's work on black holes led to a deeper understanding of these cosmic phenomena and their role in the universe's evolution.","The book's accessibility and clarity helped bridge the gap between science and popular culture, making complex concepts more engaging for the general public."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"stephen-hawking","sourceName":"Stephen Hawking","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-brief-history-of-time","targetName":"A Brief History of Time","context":"Published in 1988"},{"sourceSlug":"black-hole","sourceName":"Black Hole","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"stephen-hawking","targetName":"Stephen Hawking","context":"In his work on black holes"},{"sourceSlug":"universe","sourceName":"Universe","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-brief-history-of-time","targetName":"A Brief History of Time","context":"In Hawking's no-boundary proposal"},{"sourceSlug":"einstein","sourceName":"Albert Einstein","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-brief-history-of-time","targetName":"A Brief History of Time","context":"Through Hawking's work on general relativity"},{"sourceSlug":"cosmology","sourceName":"Cosmology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-brief-history-of-time","targetName":"A Brief History of Time","context":"Through Hawking's contributions to the field"},{"sourceSlug":"black-hole-singularity","sourceName":"Black Hole Singularity","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-brief-history-of-time","targetName":"A Brief History of Time","context":"In Hawking's research on Hawking radiation"},{"sourceSlug":"space-time","sourceName":"Space-Time","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"a-brief-history-of-time","targetName":"A Brief History of Time","context":"Through Hawking's work on the no-boundary proposal"}],"places":[{"name":"Cambridge, England","role":"Place of publication"},{"name":"University of Cambridge","role":"Institution associated with Stephen Hawking's research"},{"name":"Oxford University Press","role":"Publisher of A Brief History of Time"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Brief History of Time","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-brief-history-of-time"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q471726","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Brief_History_of_Time"],"tags":["literary work","Histories & Chronicles","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Brief History of Time contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Brief History of Time: From the Big Bang to Black Holes is the popular science book by the theoretical physicist Stephen Hawking (1942–2018), published by Bantam Books on 1 April 1988. One of the most successful popular science books ever published — selling over 25 million copies, translated into more than 40 languages, and spending 237 weeks on the Sunday Times bestseller list (a record) — it brought the concepts of modern cosmology (the Big Bang, black holes, spacetime, quantum gravity, the arrow of time, the possibility of a complete unified theory of physics) to a global popular audience with clarity, wit, and accessible prose that made it the defining popular science text of the late 20th century. Hawking's personal story — writing and speaking from a wheelchair, dictating through a computer due to motor neurone disease — gave the book an additional moral and intellectual resonance that contributed to its enormous cultural impact.

A Brief History of Time covers the development of scientific understanding of the universe from Aristotle and Ptolemy through Newton, Einstein, and the development of quantum mechanics, to the contemporary challenges of cosmology and particle physics. Its central themes include: the Big Bang as the origin of spacetime itself (not an explosion within pre-existing space, but the beginning of space and time); black holes as regions where spacetime curvature becomes infinite (and Hawking's own major contribution — that black holes emit thermal radiation through quantum effects, 'Hawking radiation'); the arrow of time and why time seems to flow only in one direction; and the 'grand unified theory' that might combine quantum mechanics and general relativity. Hawking's famous closing question — 'Why does the universe go to all the trouble of existing?' — gestures toward the philosophical and theological implications of a complete physical theory.

The book's cultural significance extends far beyond its scientific content — it established popular science writing as a major publishing genre, inspired a generation of scientists and science communicators, and made Hawking into one of the most recognisable intellectual figures of the 20th century, a symbol of the power of pure thought to transcend physical limitation.","subjects":["Physics","Modern Era","Cosmology","Popular Science","20th Century","Astrophysics","Black Holes","Stephen Hawking"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Brief History of Time's impact on modern astrophysics and our understanding of the universe has been profound, inspiring new generations of scientists and thinkers.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
